---

<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<meta charset="utf-8" />
		<meta
			name="viewport"
			content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0, maximum-scale=1.0, minimum-scale=1.0, user-scalable=no"
		/>
		<meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark" />
		<meta name="generator" content={Astro.generator} />
		<title>{name}</title>
		<Font cssVariable="--font-lexend" preload />
	</head>
	<body class="bg-background leading-relaxed mx-auto max-w-lg px-5 py-8">
		{/*
		<h1>{name}</h1>
		<ul>
			{
				socials.map(({ site, href, rel }) => (
					<li>
						<a href={href} rel={rel}>
							{site}
						</a>
					</li>
				))
			}
		</ul>
		*/}

		<h1 class="text-title">On setting type for the screen</h1>
		<p class="mt-2 text-body">
			Most of what we read on the web is set by default. The browser picks a
			size, a line height, and a measure, and the words land wherever they
			land. It works, mostly, but it rarely feels considered.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4 text-body">
			A page that is meant to be read deserves a little more care. Not
			ornament, just attention: a comfortable line length, enough space
			between lines for the eye to find its way back, and a clear sense of
			what matters most.
		</p>

		<h2 class="mt-10 text-heading">Start with the measure</h2>
		<p class="mt-2 text-body">
			The measure is the length of a line of text. Too long and the reader
			loses their place on the return trip; too short and the rhythm turns
			choppy. Somewhere around sixty to seventy characters is a good place to
			begin, and the rest of the page can be built around it.
		</p>

		<h3 class="mt-6 text-subheading">A note on line height</h3>
		<p class="mt-2 text-body">
			Line height and measure work together. Longer lines want a little more
			leading so the eye can track back to the start of the next line. Shorter
			lines can afford to be set tighter.
		</p>

		<h3 class="mt-6 text-subheading">Hierarchy without noise</h3>
		<p class="mt-2 text-body">
			Headings should guide, not shout. A modest change in size or weight is
			usually enough to signal a new section, especially when the body text
			around it is calm and consistent.
		</p>

		<h2 class="mt-10 text-heading">Then let the color recede</h2>
		<p class="mt-2 text-body">
			Text and background should sit comfortably together in both light and
			dark. Near-black on near-white, and the reverse, with nothing competing
			for attention. The words do the work.
		</p>
	</body>
</html>
